    "content": "The Federal Government of Somalia has unveiled a national policy designed to strengthen the protection of civilians during military operations and armed conflicts. The official launch took place during a ceremony where Defense Minister Ahmed Macallin Fiqi presented the policy, marking a significant step in the country's efforts to align its military practices with international humanitarian standards.\n\nThe policy outlines measures to safeguard the lives, dignity, and rights of civilians, emphasizing the importance of minimizing harm during security operations. Minister Fiqi described civilian protection as a national and constitutional obligation, reiterating the government's commitment to upholding these principles in all military engagements. The event was attended by representatives from the federal government, regional administrations, diplomats, and various relevant organizations.\n\nThis development comes amid ongoing military operations against insurgent groups in Somalia, which have often resulted in civilian casualties and displacement. The new policy aims to address these concerns by establishing clear guidelines for the conduct of armed forces and security agencies during operations. It is expected to enhance accountability and ensure that civilian populations are not inadvertently targeted or harmed.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this policy represents a move toward greater stability and human rights observance in their homeland. Many diaspora members have voiced concerns over the safety of relatives in conflict zones, and this policy could alleviate some of those worries. It also reflects progress in the broader state-building process, signaling a commitment to rule of law and sustainable peace.\n\nThe implementation of this policy will likely involve training programs for military personnel, monitoring mechanisms, and collaboration with international partners. While challenges remain, the adoption of this policy is a positive step toward protecting vulnerable communities and rebuilding trust between citizens and state institutions.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/05/soomaaliya-oo-daahfurtay-siyaasad-qaran-oo-lagu-xoojinayo-ilaalinta-rayidka-xilliyada-hawlgallada-milatari/",
