    "id": "news-1788610446937-3",
    "title": "Somalia Joins African Continental Free Trade Area as 50th Member",
    "original_title": "Soomaaliya oo si rasmi ah ugu biirtay AfCFTA iyo ganacsatada Soomaaliyeed oo helaya Suuq ballaaran",
    "slug": "somalia-joins-african-continental-free-trade-area-as-50th-member",
    "summary": "Somalia has officially joined the African Continental Free Trade Area (AfCFTA), becoming the 50th member state. The move grants Somali businesses access to a vast market of over 1.3 billion people across Africa.",
    "content": "Somalia has formally completed its accession to the African Continental Free Trade Area (AfCFTA), following the submission of its ratification documents to the African Union in Addis Ababa. This step makes Somalia the 50th country to join the continental trade bloc, a milestone that many observers view as a significant commitment to regional economic integration.\n\nThe ratification process began domestically, with the Federal Parliament approving the agreement and President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud signing it into law. The handover of the instruments in Addis Ababa marks the final administrative step, bringing Somalia into the fold of African nations working to eliminate trade barriers and boost intra-African commerce.\n\nFor Somali businesses, this development opens up new opportunities to access a market of over 1.3 billion people with a combined GDP of roughly $3.4 trillion. By participating in AfCFTA, Somali traders and producers can potentially expand their exports beyond traditional partners, tapping into growing economies across the continent. The agreement is designed to reduce tariffs and non-tariff barriers, making cross-border trade easier and more predictable.\n\nThe Somali diaspora, known for its entrepreneurial spirit and global networks, may find particular value in this new arrangement. Many diaspora-owned enterprises maintain strong links with their homeland and could leverage AfCFTA to establish or scale operations in other African countries. This integration might also encourage investment flows into Somalia, as external partners view the country’s commitment to continental trade frameworks positively.\n\nChallenges remain, including the need to improve domestic infrastructure, harmonize customs procedures, and build capacity among local firms to meet regional standards. However, the government has signalled its intention to collaborate with the private sector to ensure Somali businesses can fully utilise the benefits of membership. For the diaspora, this represents a chance to participate in the continent’s economic transformation while supporting Somalia’s development trajectory.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/05/soomaaliya-oo-si-rasmi-ah-ugu-biirtay-afcfta-iyo-ganacsatada-soomaaliyeed-oo-helaya-suuq-ballaaran/",
